"""Original concise educational summaries checked against OpenStax chapters.

Two distinct aspects per chapter, below 200 derived words per source including
prompts. These are 24 additional explanations, not 24 independent textbooks.
"""
B='https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/'
C='https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/'
P='https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/'
SCIENCE=[
('isotopes',B+'2-1-atoms-isotopes-ions-and-molecules-the-building-blocks','How can atoms of one element have different masses?',
 'An element is identified by its number of protons. Isotopes of that element have the same proton count but different numbers of neutrons, so their masses differ. Changing the neutron count does not by itself turn the atom into another element.\n\nFor example, carbon isotopes all contain six protons. Their differing neutron counts account for their different mass numbers.'),
('ions',B+'2-1-atoms-isotopes-ions-and-molecules-the-building-blocks','Explain how an atom becomes a positive or negative ion.',
 'An atom becomes an ion when its electron count no longer balances its proton count. Losing electrons leaves a net positive charge; gaining electrons gives a net negative charge.\n\nThis changes the electrical charge without changing which element the atom is. Element identity depends on the number of protons, which electron transfer does not change.'),
('diffusion',B+'5-2-passive-transport','Does diffusion stop all molecular motion at equilibrium?',
 'No. Molecules continue moving randomly at equilibrium. Before equilibrium, random motion produces a net spread from higher concentration toward lower concentration.\n\nAt equilibrium there is no overall concentration gradient driving a net transfer in one direction. Individual molecules still move both ways. The absence of net movement is different from every molecule becoming motionless.'),
('osmosis',B+'5-2-passive-transport','Explain osmosis across a membrane that passes water but not the dissolved solute.',
 'Osmosis is the net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane. When pressure is initially equal, water tends to move toward the side with more dissolved solute that cannot cross the membrane.\n\nThe solute need not cross for this to happen. Net water movement can change the volumes and pressures on the two sides, eventually reaching a balance.'),
('nucleotide',B+'3-5-nucleic-acids','What makes up a nucleotide, and how does it relate to DNA?',
 'A nucleotide contains a sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogen-containing base. DNA is a polymer built from linked nucleotides.\n\nThe sequence of bases carries genetic information. The sugar and phosphate form the repeating backbone, while the bases provide the varying sequence. A nucleotide is therefore a building block of DNA, not a complete chromosome by itself.'),
('rna',B+'3-5-nucleic-acids','Compare the basic roles of messenger RNA and transfer RNA.',
 'Messenger RNA carries a nucleotide sequence that can be read during protein synthesis. Transfer RNA helps connect that sequence to amino acids: it pairs with an appropriate codon and delivers the corresponding amino acid.\n\nThese RNAs perform different jobs. Messenger RNA provides the sequence being translated; transfer RNA helps assemble the amino-acid chain in the specified order.'),
('catalysis',B+'6-5-enzymes','How can an enzyme speed up a reaction without supplying its overall energy?',
 'An enzyme lowers the activation barrier for a reaction. More reacting molecules can then follow the reaction pathway in a given time.\n\nThe enzyme does not change the overall free-energy difference between reactants and products. It speeds the approach to equilibrium rather than making an energetically unfavorable reaction favorable merely by being present.'),
('specificity',B+'6-5-enzymes','Why do enzymes act on particular substrates rather than every molecule?',
 'An enzyme has an active site whose shape and chemical properties favor interactions with particular substrates. Binding can involve adjustments in shape, often described as induced fit.\n\nA molecule that cannot make the appropriate interactions is not efficiently processed by that enzyme. Specificity depends on molecular recognition and chemistry, not on an enzyme choosing what the cell needs.'),
('mitosis',B+'10-2-the-cell-cycle','Distinguish DNA replication from mitosis.',
 'DNA replication makes copies of the genetic material before division. Mitosis then separates the duplicated chromosomes into two nuclei.\n\nThey are different events in the cell cycle. Copying DNA alone does not divide the nucleus, and chromosome separation must distribute the copies correctly so each daughter nucleus receives the appropriate genetic material.'),
('cytokinesis',B+'10-2-the-cell-cycle','How does cytokinesis differ from mitosis?',
 'Mitosis divides the nuclear genetic material into daughter nuclei. Cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm, separating the cell into daughter cells.\n\nThe distinction matters because nuclear division and physical separation of the cell are not the same operation. Both normally contribute to producing separate daughter cells during a typical mitotic cell cycle.'),
('selection',B+'18-1-understanding-evolution','Does natural selection make an individual evolve because it needs a new trait?',
 'Natural selection acts on heritable variation among individuals. If some variants leave more surviving offspring in a particular environment, those variants can become more common over generations.\n\nAn individual does not acquire the required inherited trait simply by needing it. Evolution describes changes in populations across generations, not a deliberate improvement chosen by each organism.'),
('variation',B+'18-1-understanding-evolution','Why is inherited variation necessary for natural selection?',
 'Selection can change a population only if individuals differ in ways that affect reproductive success and some of those differences are inherited. Offspring can then carry the variants associated with greater reproductive success.\n\nWithout relevant inherited differences, differential survival alone cannot produce that evolutionary change. A useful variant may also be advantageous in one environment but not in another.'),
('covalent',C+'7-2-covalent-bonding','What is shared in a covalent bond?',
 'A covalent bond involves shared electron density between atoms. Attraction between the electrons and both nuclei can stabilize the bonded arrangement.\n\nThe atoms do not merge their nuclei. Their nuclei remain distinct, and the bond has a characteristic distance that reflects the balance of attractive and repulsive interactions.'),
('polarity',C+'7-2-covalent-bonding','Does a covalent bond always share electrons equally?',
 'No. If the bonded atoms attract electrons differently, the shared electron density is distributed unequally. This produces a polar covalent bond, with partial charges at its ends.\n\nUnequal sharing differs from treating the electrons as completely transferred to form separate ions. Bond polarity depends on the difference in the atoms\' tendencies to attract shared electrons.'),
('energy',P+'9-2-mechanical-energy-and-conservation-of-energy','Does friction destroy energy when a moving object slows down?',
 'Friction can reduce an object\'s mechanical energy while increasing internal energy in the object and its surroundings. The energy is transformed rather than destroyed.\n\nConservation of total energy does not mean that mechanical energy must stay constant in every situation. To account for the change, include the forms of energy and parts of the system that receive the transferred energy.'),
('potential',P+'9-2-mechanical-energy-and-conservation-of-energy','Describe the energy changes of an ideal frictionless roller coaster descending a hill.',
 'As the car descends, its gravitational potential energy decreases and its kinetic energy increases. It moves faster as stored gravitational energy is converted into motion.\n\nIn the ideal case with no friction, air resistance, or external energy input, the total mechanical energy remains constant. The individual kinetic and potential contributions can change while their sum stays the same.'),
('momentum',P+'8-1-linear-momentum-force-and-impulse','Why is momentum not determined by speed alone?',
 'Linear momentum equals mass times velocity. Two objects moving at the same speed can therefore have different momentum magnitudes if their masses differ.\n\nDirection also matters because velocity and momentum are vectors. An object moving east and an otherwise identical object moving west have oppositely directed momenta, even though their speeds are equal.'),
('impulse',P+'8-1-linear-momentum-force-and-impulse','Why can increasing stopping time reduce average stopping force?',
 'Impulse equals the change in momentum. For a fixed momentum change, the average net force multiplied by the stopping time has the same value.\n\nSpreading that change over a longer time reduces the required average force. The momentum still changes by the same amount; the longer stopping process changes the rate of that change.'),
('gas-volume',C+'9-2-relating-pressure-volume-amount-and-temperature-the-ideal-gas-law','What happens to ideal-gas pressure if volume is halved at fixed temperature and amount?',
 'The pressure doubles. For a fixed amount of ideal gas at constant temperature, pressure times volume remains constant.\n\nHalving the volume therefore requires doubling the pressure to preserve that product. The conditions matter: if temperature or the amount of gas also changes, the simple inverse relationship alone is insufficient.'),
('gas-temperature',C+'9-2-relating-pressure-volume-amount-and-temperature-the-ideal-gas-law','Why must the ideal gas law use absolute temperature?',
 'The ideal gas law relates pressure, volume, amount of gas, and absolute temperature. Temperature must be measured on an absolute scale such as kelvin for the proportional relationship to apply.\n\nAt fixed volume and amount of gas, doubling the kelvin temperature doubles the pressure. Doubling a Celsius reading generally does not double the absolute temperature.'),
('foodweb',B+'46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems','Why is a food web often more realistic than a single food chain?',
 'A food chain shows one sequence of feeding relationships. A food web connects multiple such pathways because organisms can eat more than one kind of food and can have several consumers.\n\nThe web represents those connections together. Removing or changing one population can therefore affect several pathways rather than just the next organism in a single line.'),
('decomposers',B+'46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems','What role do decomposers have in an ecosystem?',
 'Decomposers process material from dead organisms and wastes. Their activity helps return nutrients to forms that can be used again within the ecosystem.\n\nThis links dead organic material to the wider food web. Without such recycling, important materials would remain locked in accumulated remains rather than returning through biological and environmental pathways.'),
('current',P+'19-1-ohms-law','Distinguish electric current from the amount of electric charge.',
 'Electric current measures how rapidly charge passes a point: charge transferred divided by elapsed time. Its unit is the ampere, equivalent to one coulomb per second.\n\nCharge is the amount being transferred, while current is its rate of transfer. Moving the same charge in less time produces a larger average current.'),
('resistance',P+'19-1-ohms-law','For an ohmic resistor, how does doubling resistance affect current at fixed voltage?',
 'Ohm\'s law gives current as voltage divided by resistance. If voltage stays fixed and resistance doubles, current is halved.\n\nThis statement assumes an ohmic component under conditions where the stated resistance applies. Voltage, resistance, and current describe different quantities; a larger resistance does not create a larger current when the applied voltage is unchanged.'),
]
